  <content>

    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Question #3: Interpret Slope and y-Intercept</title>
        <media alt="">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_07_01.jpg"/>
        </media>
      <para id="para-00001">The figure shows the graph of the equation used to convert temperature in Celsius to
        temperature in Fahrenheit: \(F = 9/5 C + 32\).</para>
      <para id="para-00002">\(F = 9/5C + 32\) is used to convert temperatures, \(C\), on the Celsius scale to
        temperatures, \(F\), on the Fahrenheit scale. Identify and interpret the slope and
        \(F\)-intercept.</para>
    </section>

    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Interpret Slope and \(y\)-Intercept </title>
      <para id="para-00003">Many real-world applications are modeled by linear equations. Often, when a linear
        equation model uses real-world data, different letters are used for the variables, instead
        of using only \(x\) and \(y\). The variable names remind us of what quantities are being
        measured.</para>
      <para id="para-00004">The equation \(T = \frac14n + 40\) is used to estimate the temperature in degrees
        Fahrenheit, \(T\), based on the number of cricket chirps, \(n\), in one minute. The graph
        shows the relationship.</para>

        <media alt="graph y=¼ x+40, x axis from 0 to 48 label x axis as n, y axis from 0 to 55 label as T">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_08_01.jpg"/>
        </media>
        
      <para id="para-00005">What do the slope and \(T\)-intercept represent?</para>
      <para id="para-00006">Answer: The slope is \(\frac14\) and represents that for every 4 chirps per minute, the
        temperature increases by 1 degree. The \(T\)- intercept means when there are zero chirps,
        the temperature is 40 degrees.</para>
      </section>

      <note class="try" id="note-00001">
      <title> Try It: Interpret Slope and \(y\)-intercepts </title>
      <para id="para-00007">Loreen has a calligraphy business. The equation \(C = 1.8n + 35\) models the relation
        between her weekly cost, \(C\), in dollars and the number of wedding invitations, \(n\),
        that she writes. The graph shows the relationship.</para>
        <media alt="art the graph of y=1.8x+35, x from 0-16 labeled n, the \(y\)-axis from 0 to 60 labeled C">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_08_02.jpg"/>
        </media>

      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00008">What do the slope and \(C\)-intercept represent?</para>
          <para id="para-00009">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00010">Here is how to interpret the slope and \(C\)-intercept:</para>
          <para id="para-00011">The slope is 1.8 and represents the cost increases by $1.80 for 1 invitation. The
            \(C\)-intercept means the cost for zero invitations is $35.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </note>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--inserted
      mini review question Begin -->
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00003">
        <exercise id="_7d1e11f8-758a-414f-a89f-9643bafb909c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00012">Sam drives a delivery van. The equation \(C = 0.5m + 60\) models the relation
              between his weekly cost, \(C\), in dollars and the number of miles, \(m\), that he
              drives. The graph shows the relationship.</para>
            <para id="para-00013">Find and interpret the meaning of the slope and \(C\)-intercept.</para>
              <media alt="">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_08_03.jpg"/>
              </media>
                
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00014">Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The slope is the change in \(C\) over
              the change in \(m\). \(\frac{0.5}1\). Looking at the graph, the \(C\)-intercept, 60,
              is when the miles are 0.  The answer is the slope, 0.5, means that the weekly cost,
              \(C\), increases by $0.50 when the number of miles driven, \(m\), increases by 1. The
              \(C\)-intercept means that when the number of miles driven is 0, the weekly cost is
              $60.</para>
              <para id="para-00015">Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The slope is the change in 𝐶
                over the change in 𝑚
               . The answer is the slope, 0.5, means that the weekly cost, 𝐶
               , increases by $0.50 when the number of miles driven, 𝑚
               , increases by 1. The 𝐶
               -intercept means that when the number of miles driven is 0, the weekly cost is $60.</para>
              <para id="para-00016">Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The slope is positive so the cost will increase per mile. The 𝐶
                -intercept is the weekly cost when the miles are 0. The answer is the slope, 0.5, means that the weekly cost, 𝐶
                , increases by $0.50 when the number of miles driven, 𝑚
                , increases by 1. The 𝐶
                -intercept means that when the number of miles driven is 0, the weekly cost is $60.</para>
              <para id="para-00017">The slope, 0.5, means that the weekly cost, 𝐶
                , increases by $0.50 when the number of miles driven, 𝑚
                , increases by 1. The 𝐶
                -intercept means that when the number of miles driven is 0, the weekly cost is $60.</para>
              <para id="para-00018">That’s correct! Check yourself: For every mile that Sam drives, the cost increases $0.50. The weekly cost is the 𝐶
                -intercept which is $60, when zero miles are driven.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--inserted
      mini review question END -->
